  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/nothobranchius_cardinalis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Nothobranchs are in general extremely beautiful fish, but this species is even more beautiful due to its deep red colours and minute size. The species has been known in the hobby for quite a long time. In Aqualog \"Killifishes of the World – Old World Killis II\" it is listed under the name Nothobranchius sp. aff. rubripinnis. It was formally described as Nothobranchius cardinalis in 2008.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/nothobranchius_cardinalis_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species originates from Tanzania, where it inhabits periodically dry tributaries of the Mbwemkuru river. These killifish are typically annual fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/nothobranchius_cardinalis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Nothobranchius: from ancient Greek \"nothos\" = false, untrue and \"branchion\" = gill, referring to the gill anatomy. cardinalis: in allusion to the blood red garment of cardinals. rubripinnis: Latin, means \"with red fins\".</p>"
    }
  ],
